    <regex pattern="int get hashCode => Object\.hash\(progressIntervalSecs, meteredWarnBytes\);"/>

    <!-- `PlaybackTrackRef.hashCode`'s argument order (M4). The fourth instance,
         and by now the shape is the point rather than the class: **every
         hand-written two-or-more-argument `Object.hash` in this tree produces
         an unkillable argument-swap mutant**, for the reason set out under
         `PosterKey` above — `a == b implies a.hashCode == b.hashCode` holds
         under every permutation of the same fields, and unequal values stay
         unequal under every permutation. The only golden value available is
         `Object.hash` itself, which is the implementation in the test file.
         (Not because it would be unstable — that claim sat here until M4.R/G2
         measured it and found it false; see the `SavedServer` entry above.)

         Here both fields are `String`, so the swap merely exchanges the hashes
         of `('1', 'English')` and `('English', '1')`. `==` compares both and
         its field order IS killed — `playback_host_test` compares refs
         differing in one field at a time.

         Cost measured with `--dry -v`: **8 mutations without this line, 6 with
         it — two, not one.** A first draft of this comment said one, on the
         reasoning that the `==` above is a single line and could not spill into
         this statement. Wrong, and in exactly the way the `PosterKey` note
         warns about: the `--dry -v` diff shows the second casualty is the
         `&&`-negation at line 23 running into the excluded text —

             other is PlaybackTrackRef && other.id == id &&!( other.label == label;

         — which does not compile and was killed by the compiler rather than by
         an assertion. Three of the four entries in this family now have this
         same second casualty. Do not predict it; diff it.

         RETIREMENT CONDITION: as the others — the day this class stops needing
         a hand-written `hashCode`. -->
    <regex pattern="int get hashCode => Object\.hash\(id, label\);"/>

    <!-- `MpvPlayer.setProperty`'s PARAMETER NAMES, in an abstract declaration.
         Not a call — a signature: `Future<void> setProperty(String name,
         String value);`. The argument-swap rule rewrites it to
         `setProperty(String value, String name)`, which is a rename of two
         positional parameters of the SAME type on a method nobody calls by
         name. Dart matches positional parameters by position, so every
         implementation still overrides it, every call site still compiles, and
         the program is byte-for-byte identical in behaviour. There is no input
         that separates the two versions, so no test can.

         `RealMpvPlayer.setProperty`'s BODY is a different matter and is
         exercised: `real_mpv_player_test` sets `tls-verify` to `yes` and to
         `no` and reads each back off a real mpv context, which is the property
         D10 rests on.

         Cost measured with `--dry -v`: **3 mutations without this line, 2
         with**. Exactly one, and it is the swap.

         RETIREMENT CONDITION: the day this port stops needing a generic
         property setter. It exists for `tls-verify` and nothing else, and a
         narrower `setVerifyTls({required bool verify})` would take one named
         parameter, which this rule cannot match at all. That is the right
         change; it is not this milestone's, because it moves the yes/no
         conversion across three files and two fakes. -->
    <regex pattern="Future&lt;void&gt; setProperty\(String name, String value\);"/>

    <!-- ++ and -- : the builtin rule rewrites them into each other via the
         arithmetic rules and produces mostly false positives on index maths.
         Retires when the resume engine's index arithmetic is expressed without
         them (it currently is — this is inherited from the builtin default and
         may be dropped at M1.9 if no survivor depends on it). -->
    <regex pattern="\+\+"/>
    <regex pattern="--"/>
  </exclude>

  <threshold failure="100">
    <rating over="99.9" name="A"/>
    <rating over="90" name="B"/>
    <rating over="75" name="C"/>
    <rating over="50" name="D"/>
    <rating over="0" name="F"/>
  </threshold>
</mutations>
